A||||12||2birds||V-6 experts, please input...Re-installed heads, runs really rough;  Ideas anyone?||||1||
Z||000000||2birds||08-20-2001||01:45 PM||||After getting my heads rebuilt, I installed them only to find the engine sounds like a Harley on acid.  <P>First, I checked the firing order, and it is set per the Haynes and 60 degree V-6 handbook, 1-2-3-4-5-6- clockwise, with the rear bank 1-3-5 from right to left.  No problem there.<P>Second, timing is set at 10 degees, same as I've always had it.  I have spark going to each cylinder.  Hurts like hell when the cap arcs to your hand...<P>Third, I checked the injector harness.  I have 15.6 ohms for each injector (checked each one prior to re-assembly), and about 6 ohms when checked at the connector (three injectors in parallel).  Makes sense according to the electronics world.  I also have 12 volts at the fuse side of the injectors (hot in run).<P>Fourth, I checked the compression.  All had about 130-150 pounds in them, except #5, which was zero.  I pulled the valve cover and rechecked the valve lash per the haynes manual, with #1 at TDC and after 360 degrees crank rotation.  Now I have the same compression in #5.  After restart, it still runs just as bad, like a Harley on crack now.<BR>Checked all vacuum lines connected, so cuts that I can see.  I don't think any can be switched, except maybe the two at the throttle body (only if you force them).<P>No check engine light or trouble codes set.<P>I know it needs new wires (found a few cuts in them) but it ran like a top before  pulled the heads, so I don't think that is the source.<P>Compression is fine on the forward bank, so I didn't recheck the valve lash there.  Valve covers are a PITA with the intake on.  That's probably my next step, unless anyone has any better ideas.<P>The only good news is that the #@$@#% exhaust leak that started this whole mess is gone now. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||08-20-2001||02:03 PM||||I would go thru them all again.<BR>Also paying attention as to which is which (intake and exhaust).||||reg||1
Z||000002||2birds||08-20-2001||10:13 PM||||Thanks, Indy resto.  That's where I will start tomorrow.  Today was lawn care day.  Best way to lower the ol' blood pressure after wrenching on the Plastic Poncho for a couple days straight.  <BR>Definitely NOT looking forward to pulling the forward rocker arm cover.  If somebody made a 1/2" spacer plate to put between the lower and intermediate manifold sections a la 5.0 Fords), it would be so much easier.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fix||08-20-2001||10:26 PM||||definitely not my area of expertise, but I'll pitch in my 2 cents.   could it be that your timing is 180 degrees off?  I would think that the car wouldn't even start if that were the case, but is it possible that you set the timing on the exhaust stroke instead of the compression stroke?  Just a thought.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||recluse||08-20-2001||11:19 PM||||I would try a new wire set.  It sounds like you are going to need them anyways if they are all cut up.  A friend of mine had that problem on his supra, he had to replace the head and we could never get it running right after getting it back together, and we fought with it for a couple weeks, checking and rechecking and resetting until he put new wires on it and ran great... we were pretty amazed||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||08-21-2001||12:39 AM||||I got a set of Wells wires at Wally World for $18.  My Taylor wires have been on for 7 years now; it's a shame they are cut, but I don't want to wait for a new set from Summit.<P>My ignition is firing #1 at TDC (compression buil-up, just before the exhaust valve opens) on #1.  If you look at the distributor as a compass, with the flat side running East-West on the bottom, the distributor rotor points at 90 degrees, and I fire 1-2-3-4-5-6 clockwise from there.  The groove in the balancer doesn't really look any wider at the TDC point for #1 cylinder than the other two grooves, but that's where the timing light shows it firing.<P>Nothing else was worked on except pulling the heads, seating the valves and removing the broken exhaust manifold bolt, and re-assembling it.  New seals and gaskets/hardware were used, too.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Joe Torma||08-21-2001||12:49 AM||||Wait a minute...your 1st post said that the rear bank was 1-3-5?  Do you mean the firewall side?  #1 is trunkside.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||2birds||08-21-2001||01:01 AM||||Rear as in back of the car, trunk side, aft starboard is #1, aft port is #5 (getting nautical  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">).  1-3-5 from right to left, looking toward the front of the car.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Joe Torma||08-21-2001||01:10 AM||||Aye Aye Cap'n<BR>Nevermind.<BR>I usually point the rotor (and position the #1wire) at #1 cyl.<p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 08-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||2birds||08-21-2001||10:22 PM||||I reset the valve lash per the manuals tonight, before it got dark.  None seemed to be off by more than 1/2 turn of the adjuster nut.  Put the rocker arm covers back on just as the mosquitos got too bad, and had to call it a night.  I'll torque the covers on and hook everything back up tomorrow morning and give it a whirl.  New plug wires, too.  Wish me luck .  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||2birds||08-22-2001||10:12 PM||||Joe Torma,<BR>You may have been correct.  I had the 3 and 5 cylinder plug wires crossed.  I put on a new set of wires this morning and noticed it then. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">  <BR>Started it up after re-assembly, purrs like a kitten.  set timing at 10 degrees, and everything was great.  For a while.  It wants to idle anywhere from 1500-2500 rpm now.  Got a check engine light briefly while I was running around enjoying it, and it set a code 35, IAC according to Haynes.<P>This has turned out to be one expensive exhaust manifold gasket repair... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mark||08-22-2001||10:45 PM||||Now it sounds like a classic vacuum leak.  Check EGR tube and all hose connections carefully.  I'm thinkin that the IAC error code is a result of a vacuum leak, not a bad IAC.||||reg||1
Z||000012||StuGood||08-23-2001||12:20 PM||||Didya forget to plug the vacuum line into the EGR actuator?  Just a guess... ||||reg||5
